ans and ranges  associated with these controls depending  on  whether or not the laboratory has  historical data  available for those particular qc  materials  so for example since the imprecision of  an  assay is an innate characteristic of the  test  regardless of the quality control  material  if there is historic qc data available  for that control  and that control has performed well  without issues in the past  the established sd or standard deviation  can be applied  to the new qc material if that target  level is similar between the two lots  so the mean of the new qc lot can be  determined using 10 measurements  ideally on separate days and then you  can apply that historic sd value around  the new mean  in most cases though especially for  sarsko v2 controls there's  no historic qc data available because  these products are just so  new so in that case new control means  and sd values have to be established  prior to  implementation of the control material  and as a standard this is usually  established  by measuring 10 uh 10 points  ideally those are acquired on separate  days  to account for as much variability as  possible and then these 20 points can be  plotted on a levy jennings chart to look  for any drifts or shifts or outliers  and if none are observed all of those  points can be used to establish the mean  and sd of that control material and so  this is just an example  of what our 20 points look like for the  saracare positive control material  that was run on the orthoclinical igg  chemiluminescent  assay and then oftentimes because of the  potential limited reliability of  the initially established sd values  we'll  often reevaluate the mean and sd about  one to two months into running the  controls this really allows us to take  into account  additional procedural procedural  variations  and factors like use of different  calibrators different reagent lots  any preventative maintenance and so on  so we have a much larger  data set with which to work with that  would lead to a more realistic  and a more accurate sd  and then finally you'll obviously want  to monitor your qc values over time  often again using levy jennings charts  and applying  the various westgard rules including  ideally use of both single event qc  rules which will detect kind of large  shifts in the assay  or or i should say and using  counting qc rules which will detect  smaller shifts or trends in the assay  over time  so for for example in our lab we  routinely look at 1 3 s 2 2  s and 4 1 s failures or trends for  for our tests and so that leads me  into a very brief kind of case study  that involved a control for one of our  stars kobe 2 serologic tests again  kind of underscoring the importance of  monitoring qc over time  so um we implemented a third party qc  material for our total antibody sars  cody2 assay  and after about a month we noticed that  the signal to cutoff ratio for that  positive control material was trending  uh downward on all of our kobas e 801  instruments and you can  you can see kind of a bird's eye view of  that in these charts  so we performed an investigation to see  what was going on and found that  for the most part the negative control  signal to cut off levels remained  uh remains stable we also retested  previously positive patient samples and  found that those signal to cutoff values  were stable and  within the expected imprecision of the  assay  and then also prior to implementation of  the vended  control material we were using pooled  positive and negative  patient controls and so we pulled those  out of the freezer  and tested those and similarly found  that the signal to cut off  ratios remained within the expected  range so  our conclusion was ultimately that we  were probably seeing  some sort of degradation of our positive  control material  despite it still being within with  instability  and i will note that this was not the  bio red control but we ultimately moved  to  using an alternative vended third party  control  um but again this is just one example of  why you'd want to monitor the numerical  values of your controls even if the  results you're reporting are simply  qualitative because  as you can imagine over time this would  lead to failed runs and lots of  unnecessary look backs and investigation  so to conclude just some general  suggestions for  antibody related quality control  third-party vendor  control material is often preferred  similar to  the situation for other assays in order  to kind of independently monitor  the performance of your of your tests  for qualitative assays which are based  off of a cut off threshold you  again need two levels of control with  the positive one ideally being  near that qualitative thrush threshold  and you'll want to establish and verify  your own control ranges  the frequency of control testing will be  dependent  on the method you use as well as the  laboratory workflow but it's typically  more frequent than  the uh just once for 24 hours  and then finally just remember the  importance of monitoring  your qc for trending over time in order  to avoid  potentially unnecessary assay  control failures and so with that  i will thank you for your attention and  turn it back to susie  and thank you dr thiel for that great  presentation  we will now start the q and a portion of  the webinar  if you have any questions you want to  ask to either dr  newton or dr thiel please do so now just  click  on that ask a question box located on  the far  left of your screen so let's get started  our first question is for you dr newton  what is the purpose of including genomic  human dna in stars cov2 control  thanks susie it's a really good question  and  the there are a variety of assays that  are available including  the original cdc sars kov2 assay  that use human targets as internal  controls for their assay  so those target genes like the  rnasp gene are present in that genomic  material  and so those would 