signed  such that qualitative results are  determined  based on an established signal to cut  off or index value  threshold and so anything above or below  that threshold is resulted as positive  or negative respectively  and so for such tests clsi recommends  that at least two control levels be used  one above  and one below that threshold and then  ideally that positive control level  should be near that qualitative cutoff  or qualitative threshold since at that  level  the control is much more likely to  detect an out of control situation  compared to a positive control which  with a much higher  relative level of that analyte so  choosing  what that control level is or can be can  be tricky though because you don't want  it to be too close or at that cut off  threshold since that might lead to  control failures and unnecessary  rejection of runs  so to overcome this we typically  consider the assay precision  characteristics that we've established  during the verification and then choose  a control level  that's about two to three fold above  that cut off threshold  and then i also just wanted to mention  that along these lines the cap  immunology checklist has a requirements  uh specifically stating that that  qualitative cutoff threshold needs to be  verified initially  after implementation and then every six  months thereafter  um and depending on the assay while some  have  calibrators that are either near or used  to determine that threshold  another way to satisfy this requirement  is to use  controls that are near that near that  cutoff  so when it comes to establishing the  control material  target value and range there's a couple  of things to keep in mind  first you know while some vendors may  provide already  pre-assigned values and ranges for their  controls  those should really be used as more of a  guide and not a replacement for  individual  laboratory established control means and  ranges  and then there are a couple of ways to  determine the means and ranges  associated with these controls depending  on  whether or not the laboratory has  historical data  available for those particular qc  materials  so for example since the imprecision of  an  assay is an innate characteristic of the  test  regardless of the quality control  material  if there is historic qc data available  for that control  and that control has performed well  without issues in the past  the established sd or standard deviation  can be applied  to the new qc material if that target  level is similar between the two lots  so the mean of the new qc lot can be  determined using 10 measurements  ideally on separate days and then you  can apply that historic sd value around  the new mean  in most cases though especially for  sarsko v2 controls there's  no historic qc data available because  these products are just so  new so in that case new control means  and sd values have to be established  prior to  implementation of the control material  and as a standard this is usually  established  by measuring 10 uh 10 points  ideally those are acquired on separate  days  to account for as much variability as  possible and then these 20 points can be  plotted on a levy jennings chart to look  for any drifts or shifts or outliers  and if none are observed all of those  points can be used to establish the mean  and sd of that control material and so  this is just an example  of what our 20 points look like for the  saracare positive control material  that was run on the orthoclinical igg  chemiluminescent  assay and then oftentimes because of the  potential limited reliability of  the initially established sd values  we'll  often reevaluate the mean and sd about  one to two months into running the  controls this really allows us to take  into account  additional procedural procedural  variations  and factors like use of different  calibrators different reagent lots  any preventative maintenance and so on  so we have a much larger  data set with which to work with that  would lead to a more realistic  and a more accurate sd  and then finally you'll obviously want  to monitor your qc values over time  often again using levy jennings charts  and applying  the various westgard rules including  ideally use of both single event qc  rules which will detect kind of large  shifts in the assay  or or i should say and using  counting qc rules which will detect  smaller shifts or trends in the assay  over time  so for for example in our lab we  routinely look at 1 3 s 2 2  s and 4 1 s failures or trends for  for our tests and so that leads me  into a very brief kind of case study  that involved a control for one of our  stars kobe 2 serologic tests again  kind of underscoring the importance of  monitoring qc over time  so um we implemented a third party qc  material for our total antibody sars  cody2 assay  and after about a month we noticed that  the signal to cutoff ratio for that  positive control material was trending  uh downward on all of our kobas e 801  instruments and you can  you can see kind of a bird's eye view of  that in these charts  so we performed an investigation to see  what was going on and found that  for the most part the negative control  signal to cut off levels remained  uh remains stable we also retested  previously positive patient samples and  found that those signal to cutoff values  were stable and  within the expected imprecision of the  assay  and then also prior to implementation of  the vended  control material we were using pooled  positive and negative  patient controls and so we pulled those  out of the freezer  and tested those and similarly found  that the signal to cut off  ratios remained within the expected  range so  our conclusion was ultimately that we  were probably seeing  some sort of degradation of our positive  control material  despite it still being within with  instability  and i will note that this was not the  bio red control but we ultimately moved  to  using an alternative vended third party  control  um but again this is just one example of  why you'd want to monitor the numerical  values of your controls even if the  results you're reporting are simply  qualitative because  as you can imagine over time this would  lead to failed runs and lots of  unnecessary look backs and investigation  so to conclude just some general  suggestions for  antibody related quality control  third-party vendor  control material is often preferred  similar to  the situation for other assays in order  to kind of independently monitor  the performance of your of your tests  for qualitative assays which are based  off of a cut off threshold you  again need two levels of control with  the positive one ideally being  near that qualitative thrush threshold  and you'll want to establish and verify  your own control ranges  the frequency of control testing will be  dependent  on the method you use as well as the  laboratory workflow but it's typically  more frequent than  the uh just once for 24 hours  and then finally just remember the  importance of monitoring  your qc for trending over time in order  to avoid  potentially unnecessary assay  control failures and so with that  i will thank you for your attention and  turn it back to susie  and thank you dr thiel for that great  presentation  we will now start the q and a portion of  the webinar  if you have any questions you want to  ask to either dr  newton or dr thiel please do so now just  click  on that ask a question box located on  the far  left of your screen so let's get started  our first question is for you dr newton  what is the purpose of including genomic  human dna in stars cov2 control  thanks susie it's a really good question  and  the there are a variety of assays that  are available including  the original cdc sars kov2 assay  that use human targets as internal  controls for their assay  so those target genes like the  rnasp gene are present in that genomic  material  and so those would be present in a  clinical specimen  and so for our external controls or any  kick controls that have genomic material  in it they would be able to detect those  targets as well i think it also has  alluded to  in my description of ideal an ideal  control material because of that genomic  material would be present in a clinical  specimen  having it part of a control would  really help to ensure that that control  mimics  what a real patient specimen would look  like  our next question is for both of you as  long as my positive  qc is positive and negative is negative  why would i worry about shifts or trends  for a qualitative test we'll start with  you dr phil  uh right so it's a great great question  because at face value right we report  positive or negative and if our controls  are positive or negative correctly then  everything should be fine right  unfortunately just a qual pause negative  doesn't really tell you much about the  analytical performance of your assay so  as in  the example case i showed earlier i  think really shows that so  technically the controls we had  qualitatively were in  they passed but clearly something was  going on  and if we didn't monitor those  quantitative values i think at some  point we  we would have reached a point where our  positive control  would have repeatedly tested negative  and we'd have to do  quite a bit of kind of backtracking and  investigation  and failing runs unnecessarily so for  that reason i think even for qualitative  tests it's helpful to  monitor those numeric values  and even if they're not necessarily  clinically significant  thank you so much dr phil and how about  you dr newton  i agree with what dr thiel said  and would just add to that you know in  her case description  she described um degradation of the  product as one  as the reason why that um those trends  went down  there are a lot of situations that could  happen  that um would impact the performance of  that material and  it really highlights not only the value  of looking at  the numbers but to actually have that  external control  as part of your assay that's carried out  over  many many lots over many many days of  you know  and even uh potentially across many  different instruments that you'd have  something that you can compare  compared to not just in a positive or  negative way but in a  purely quantitative way um to  to evaluate when those issues are or to  identify when those issues  um are coming up and so  the those those numbers as as dr thiel  said are really important because  without that you don't you don't see  that information  you don't see that change as  dramatically and as uh  obviously thank you so much  our next question is for you dr thiel is  there any guidance  available suggesting which targets are  preferred  for antibody testing i mean in terms of  nucleus kept said s1 s2  or receptor binding domains and if not  do you have any  recommendations yeah so that's a  a good question so there's  many studies now coming out right  looking at our immune response to these  various sarcove-2 proteins and  antigens but kind of at a high level  i think there's a few take home points  that we can take from those so overall  i don't actually think there's a really  meaningful difference in sensitivity  especially during the early stages of  infection between  using serologic assays that are based on  the nucleic acid  protein versus any of the spike  based protein assays you know regardless  of the test  all individuals seroconvert or not all  most  zero convert 12 to 14 days after symptom  onset  um from a longevity perspective i think  we'll we're still really learning about  how long these antibodies last  it's you know especially to the various  proteins  there's some recent studies suggesting  that maybe antibodies to the  nucleocapsid  decline faster than those to the spike  proteins but honestly i think the jury  is still out on that  and then even though it's kind of hard  to say exactly what will happen  in the future and what will be needed  once we have vaccines available  many of the vaccines are you know kind  of  a targeting or trying to stimulate an  immune response to the spike protein  in particular so it may be beneficial to  have spike based assays  on board but again how those will be  used in the future is hard to say  thank you so much dr thiel our next  question is for you dr newton  what's the best material to evaluate the  limit of detection  for my method  yeah the thing the um important thing to  recognize first is that different assays  have ultimately will have different  limits of detection  so if you have multiple platforms in  your lab that you're evaluating  you need to have a material that has  a is present in a  high concentration a high enough  concentration where you can make a wide  range of dilutions that allow you to  really  really differentiate those lods from  assay to assay  and as i mentioned in the talk you know  some of the  some of the controls that are available  from commercial manufacturers don't  don't come in high enough concentration  to allow you to make  broad dilution series especially  and when you make those dilutions you're  going to spike them into  transport media a mill or three mils of  transport media which further dilutes  the sample and and so the concentration  of that  dilution needs to be very high when you  when you're spiking it  and so that i think is the first thing  the second thing that's really important  is that the concentration of the  material is really accurate  and so there are a variety of  options that are out there that have  used droplet digital pcr  to quantify that material and the  and those numbers are are very very  accurate  and very very precise so i think those  are the  the two most important factors that  allow you to have  high concentrations that are accurately  represented  and then that finally that it's a  material  that can be stable over time that you  can  make dilutions store them and pull them  out  and test them again and get the same  get the same results because you're  often  doing these experiments on multiple days  with multiple  individuals and multiple instruments and  you'd like  to have the solutions that you're using  to make that determination be the same  in all those evaluations you're not  going back and  and repeating and adding another very  variable process  thank you so much dr newton and i want  to thank our audience for these great  questions coming in  we have time for just one more question  and it goes to you dr  seal some third-party qc vendors  provide inter-laboratory comparison  reports for their qc  are there any benefits from  participating in such a program for sars  co-lead to antibody testing  sure so there are definitely some  benefits to participating in programs  like that  because it does in some cases provide  kind of a real-time  intra-laboratory comparison or an  evaluation of how your  your controls are performing and  ultimately how your assay is  is performing so these sorts of programs  basically let the lab  see how they their assay is performing  leverage  relative to other laboratories using the  same assets and  controls um and if you find that your  controls are kind of blocking  compared to other athletes you'll be  able to see that  issue fairly quickly and take  appropriate measures to investigate the  issue before  before it becomes a major problem so  thank you so much dr dr thiel thank you  both for your time  today and for your important work and  your research before we go  i do want to thank the audience again  for joining us today and for their  interesting questions  and questions we did not have time to  address today will be answered and  posted in that event  resources by the end of next week this  webcast will be available  on demand in the auditorium from friday  on and if you found this  information informative and helpful  please share it with your colleagues  bio rad will be hosting a new qc related  webinar  every month this year next month's topic  is  how to limit patient harm from erroneous  results qc strategies based on risk  management and will be held in september  on september 16th please check back  regularly for new topics  and take time to explore our posters  booths and bio rads new covid  product showcase in the virtual  conference until next time  take care be safe and bye


